In the first code segment, there is a for loop which increments the value of `x` by one for `y` number of times. So the value of `x` is expected to be x + `y` after the for loop finishes execution. 

In the second code segment, when an item is added to the ArrayList through the `Add()` method, it checks whether the object added is unique, i.e., it is not present in the list before.

In the third code segment, when an item is removed from the ArrayList through the `RemoveAt()` method, the item which is removed is not present anywhere in the list. Hence, the expected result is that the removed item is not in the list after the operation. 

These assertions are added using the `Debug.Assert()` method, which is only triggered if the condition is false. This makes sure the execution of the program doesn't continue if there is a violation of the condition. Such violations often indicate bugs in the program. Hence, its usefulness in debugging complex programs.--------------------
